WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.196
You’re on your way to work, driving, enjoying the freedom and flexibility your car provides you.

00:00:04.196 --> 00:00:06.196
But then, BAM. Traffic jam.

00:00:06.197 --> 00:00:09.105
You inch forward on the congested 4-lane road for a good 20 minutes,

00:00:09.106 --> 00:00:11.106
thinking about how the problem of traffic could be solved.

00:00:11.107 --> 00:00:16.564
Well, of course by adding more lanes! If the road is full, you widen the road! Simple! Common sense!

00:00:16.565 --> 00:00:21.145
The city leadership agrees. The road is widened to 6 lanes, and for a few weeks or months traffic flows better,

00:00:21.145 --> 00:00:23.347
but then, BAM. Traffic jam again.

00:00:23.348 --> 00:00:27.556
The road is widened again, this time to 8 lanes, and for a few weeks or months traffic flows better,

00:00:27.557 --> 00:00:31.866
but then, BAM. Traffic jam yet again. What on Earth is going on?

00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:35.003
How come the road always fills up regardless of how many lanes we add?

00:00:35.003 --> 00:00:39.489
This phenomenon is called “induced demand”. First observed in the US in the 1930s.

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:42.462
And our politicians have been pretending it doesn’t exist ever since. 

00:00:42.463 --> 00:00:49.744
Let’s take this 4-lane road connecting a suburb with the city center. People in that suburb can either take the bike, the train, or the bus into the city. Or, they can drive.

00:00:49.745 --> 00:00:55.858
And many do, thus the road is clogged. The slowness of car traffic motivates people to seek alternatives though, like the bike, the bus or the train.

00:00:55.859 --> 00:00:59.310
Once the road widened to 8 lanes, people find that driving is faster now.

00:00:59.311 --> 00:01:06.232
And people keep switching to their cars to take advantage of this, until the road fills up again, and car traffic becomes slow, and the vicious cycle starts anew.

00:01:06.233 --> 00:01:11.703
Okay, so why don’t we try building so much road capacity that it will never be full? Wouldn’t that solve traffic?

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:17.925
No. Especially around large cities, there simply isn’t enough physical space to build enough lanes to accommodate every potential driver.

00:01:17.926 --> 00:01:22.829
For example, the Katy freeway in Texas, widened to now-26 lanes, still gets clogged up.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:27.581
We will not solve traffic by widening roads, just like we won’t solve obesity by loosening our belts.

00:01:27.582 --> 00:01:32.194
By widening the road, instead of solving traffic, you only get to choose how many lanes of traffic jam you want.

00:01:32.195 --> 00:01:33.943
So what is the solution to traffic? 

00:01:33.944 --> 00:01:40.082
Easy! Turns out induced demand also works in reverse. The less capacity a road has, the more people opt for alternatives.

00:01:40.083 --> 00:01:45.907
If lane reductions are coupled with investment into bike lanes and public transit, BAM! Suddenly traffic gets a lot less severe!

00:01:45.912 --> 00:01:53.685
Less being late from work, less finite life wasted standing bumper to bumper, less noise, less pollution, and safer adjacent streets for children and pets.

00:01:53.686 --> 00:01:56.090
Take a look at this iconic picture of the Katy freeway.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:03.719
Assuming an average US car occupancy of 1.5 per vehicle, every single person in this picture would fit inside two of these Stadler double-decker commuter trains.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.316
I know, because I counted.

00:02:05.317 --> 00:02:11.771
Now tell me: as a taxpayer, would you rather pay for the maintenance of a two-track rail line, or 26 lanes of road? Answers on a postcard.

00:02:11.772 --> 00:02:16.851
Especially if you’re watching this from the US, next time someone tells you public transit is communism or something, remember:

00:02:16.851 --> 00:02:19.671
As a European taxpayer, I pay for this.

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:22.492
As an American taxpayer, you pay for this.

00:02:22.493 --> 00:02:29.307
As the saying goes, developed country is not where everyone drives a car. It’s where nobody needs a car to get around.

